                <pb facs="1034" n="920" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg920"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.252/55: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Chile</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Culbertson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Santiago</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-08-12T16:00:00-05:00">August 12, 1931—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 5:39 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>93. Chilean Government is seriously considering the establishment of a system of
                    licensing imports and this possibility should be considered in connection with
                    my negotiations for exchange of notes suggested in Department’s telegram No. 30,
                    July 18, 2 p.m. What is the Department’s attitude toward such a practice? Is it
                    sufficient to ask treatment as favorable with respect to commodities,
                    valuations, and quantities as may be accorded to the commerce of any other
                    country? Should we adopt a special policy with reference to products which are
                    imported almost exclusively from the United States?</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Culbertson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
